  "findings": {
    "fig1": "Almaty has the highest mean PM2.5 (26.0 µg/m³) and Karaganda the lowest (14.4 µg/m³); the difference is a factor of 1.8. Mean PM2.5 is above the WHO 24-hour guideline of 15 µg/m³ in: Almaty, Shymkent.",
    "fig2": "Averaged over all cities the most polluted day is 2026-11-18 (25.0 µg/m³) and the cleanest is 2026-11-11 (10.8 µg/m³). The daily mean exceeded the WHO guideline on 12 of 14 days in Almaty, the most among the cities.",
    "fig3": "Across all cities PM2.5 is highest around 21:00 (28.0 µg/m³) and lowest around 14:00 (12.1 µg/m³). By part of the day, 'evening' is the most polluted (26.2 µg/m³) and 'afternoon' the least (14.2 µg/m³).",
    "fig4": "Almaty shows the widest spread of hourly values (std 11.9 µg/m³, maximum 60.5 µg/m³). The median PM2.5 ranges from 15.2 to 24.4 µg/m³ between the cities.",
    "fig5": "The share of hours with European AQI 'Poor' or worse is largest in Almaty (1.5 % of hours) and smallest in Astana (0.0 %).",
    "fig6": "PM2.5 is most strongly correlated with european_aqi (r = 0.92); its correlation with PM10 is 0.92, with the European AQI 0.92.",
    "conclusions": [
      "Highest average PM2.5: Almaty (26.0 µg/m³); lowest: Karaganda (14.4 µg/m³).",
      "Share of hours above the WHO 24-hour PM2.5 guideline: Almaty 81 %, Shymkent 63 %, Karaganda 51 %, Astana 51 %.",
      "Typical daily rhythm: peak near 21:00, minimum near 14:00.",
      "Strongest relation of PM2.5 to another variable: european_aqi (r = 0.92)."
